**κ = 4986133/10^11 ≈ 4.986133×10⁻⁵ > 2⁻¹⁵**, conditional on OpenAI's base theorem and the retained analytic and fixed-tape interfaces. This is **4.651% above #60** (4764513337/10^14), the highest open claim at the time of writing, **6.782% above #57** (4669442391/10^14) and **10.849% above #53**. It does not use #57's compiler or #54's clones. The exact bit saving is 4986382/10^11.

Stacked with #57's unchanged joint frame compiler, the same graph gives **κ = 5101691/10^11 ≈ 5.101691×10⁻⁵**, **7.077% above #60** and **9.257% above #57**. That second witness also depends on #57's compiler argument.

## Change and mathematical scope

This builds on #53 (`3ffd402`). The branch also carries #57's commit (`cd350f7`, by eumemic) unchanged, for the stacked witness. Only the scalar producer DAG changes. The new files are in `research/pair-assembly/`.

A role is a physical scratch wire: R = c + q − μ, where μ counts retained carrier links. A link needs the donor's original envelope inside the target's: the target's common core inside the donor's, and the donor's cover inside the target's. So R counts the additions that cannot continue a carrier, plus q. Both changes raise μ.

**1. Interval strips.** Each vertex needs every leave-one-out sum of its k strip values. #53 used s_j = A_(j−2) + (v_(j−1) + B_(j+1)). We build every cyclic interval instead, each from an interval one item shorter: I(a, m) = I(a, m−1) + v_(a+m−1), and s_j = I(j+1, k−1). Every interval shorter than k−1 can continue into I(a−1, m+1), which consumes the same last item and contains it. This costs k(k−2)+1 additions instead of about 4k, but only the k outputs and the total stay unmatched. In a model of one strip, an exact integer program over every leave-one-out circuit shows k−1 unmatched additions is the minimum for k ≤ 8. Skip-prefix leaves 2k−3.

**2. Core-aware pair assembly.** For groups (a, a2) and (b, b2), with F = far[i,j] and strip outputs Sa, Sa2, Sb, Sb2:

```
F+Sa, F+Sa2, F+Sb, F+Sb2
Y1 = (F+Sb)+Sa,       Y2 = (F+Sa)+Sb2
Y3 = (F+Sa2)+e(a,b2), Y4 = (F+Sb2)+e(a,b)
out[a,b]  = e(a2,b2)+Y1,  out[a,b2]  = e(a2,b)+Y2
out[a2,b] = Y3+Sb,        out[a2,b2] = Y4+Sa2
```

Each F+S node now continues a carrier into a later consumer of its strip operand with the same core and a larger cover. #53's assembly leaves all 10 additions per group pair unmatched; this one leaves 8. An exact integer program over every assembly of the nine pieces, with their actual cores and covers, shows 8 is optimal. Cores matter here: Sa + e(a2,b2) has the two-point core {c, a2}, so a one-point-core donor cannot continue into it.

